SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Anderson♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Michigan State University College of Human Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Michigan State University College of Human Medicine submits these comments with concern that HHS validation frameworks remain insufficient for reproducible clinical AI deployment at scale.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Torres♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Alliance for Health Policy♔♔♔♔♔♔The Alliance for Health Policy urges HHS to impose an immediate moratorium on AI reimbursement incentives until independent market-structure analysis and binding anti-concentration safeguards are in place.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Lee♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔National Patient Advocate Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔The National Patient Advocate Foundation urges HHS to pause and ask whether patients in underserved communities genuinely understand how AI tools are shaping their diagnoses before accelerating adoption any further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Jones♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Johns Hopkins Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Johns Hopkins Medicine | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Johns Hopkins Medicine submits these comments in response to the HHS request on AI adoption in clinical care, drawing on our own operational findings rather than industry projections. Our Q4 2024 technology working group identified liability exposure for non-device AI tools as the single greatest barrier to adoption across our affiliated clinics. An internal review of fourteen payer relationships found approximately $2.3 million in disputed claims tied to inconsistent coding for AI-supported services. HHS should require real-world performance validation in diverse deployment environments before reimbursement pathways open, not after the contracts are already signed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Jones♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Fifteen years on the cardiac step-down unit at Grady, and the case I keep coming back to is a young Black man, early 30s, whose chest pain kept getting triaged down because his presentation didn't match the textbook. A colleague pushed back hard. STEMI. He lived because a human being with twenty years of experience said no, something is wrong here.⏎⏎i genuinely don't know who pushes back when the algorithm says the same wrong thing, especially on a unit running short because administration decided the AI "handles it."⏎⏎the jobs being quietly eliminated in billing and prior auth, those are real jobs. mostly women, mostly workers of color. that money isn't going back to patients.⏎⏎HHS, please slow this down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Jackson♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔National Hemophilia Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Hemophilia Foundation, we urge HHS to require demonstrated real-world performance validation before any AI tool receives reimbursement incentives. Benchmark performance is not real-world performance. Our member survey documented 14 flagged AI recommendations in 24 months, and fewer than 20% of organizations had received formal evaluation guidance. That is not a readiness gap. That is recklessness.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Sanchez♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔University of Illinois Chicago School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of Illinois Chicago School of Public Health urges HHS to require prospective bias auditing standards before accelerating AI adoption, given documented reproducibility failures affecting over two-thirds of surveyed pathology practices.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Clark♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔American Civil Liberties Union♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Civil Liberties Union, HHS must condition any acceleration of clinical AI adoption on enforceable equity audits, mandatory consent disclosures, and labor impact assessments protecting frontline workers.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Perez♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister drove four hours from Tok to Fairbanks for a cardiac screening and an algorithm said she was low-risk, trained on god knows what data, and nobody told us a computer made that call. Eight months before a human caught what was actually wrong. I read about what UnitedHealth was doing with their NaviHealth system, churning out thousands of denials in seconds, and I thought, that is exactly what happened to her. Most people who get a wrong denial just stop fighting. Not because they give up on themselves, they give up because the appeals process is designed to take more time and money and energy than most sick people have, and that is especially true if you are elderly, or your first language is not English, or you live four hours from the nearest specialist like a lot of Alaskans do. HHS has the authority to require a real human being to review any AI-generated denial before it stands. If you move faster on AI adoption without doing that first, I want you to say out loud whose side you are on, because I think I already know.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Wilson♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔La Jolla Institute for Immunology♔♔♔♔♔♔La Jolla Institute for Immunology urges HHS to pause and require bias auditing standards before scaling AI clinical tools, given documented harms in pulse oximetry and sepsis prediction already affecting marginalized patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Gonzalez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Marisol spent eight months being told her symptoms were stress, anxiety, maybe just fatigue, before a specialist in Baton Rouge FINALLY caught her lupus. That was with real human doctors at every step!! What terrifies me is that HHS seems to want to SPEED UP the process of putting machines in between patients and the people who are actually responsible for their care. A good doctor notices when you're not saying everything. They read your face. They carry genuine responsibility for what happens next. An algorithm does NOT do that, no matter how accurate its outputs are. Efficiency is not the same thing as good care, and I wish someone at HHS would stop treating it like it is. Please slow down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Taylor♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Megan spent three weeks waiting for a treatment decision because an automated insurance system flagged her MRI incorrectly, and not one person caught it until her neurologist pulled the original scans herself. That doctor saved Megan from months of delay. I understand HHS wants to move faster with AI in clinical care, and I'm not saying technology has no place here, but what about the people who are supposed to catch these errors when the software is wrong? Slow down and require that a real human being remains accountable at every step before any of this goes further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Lopez♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American College of Emergency Physicians, HHS must require mandatory human review of all AI-generated coverage denials before accelerating adoption further. Automated denials are already harming the most vulnerable patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Miller♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Academy of Pediatrics♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Academy of Pediatrics, we urge HHS to require mandatory pre-deployment bias auditing with disaggregated outcome data before any AI clinical tool reaches reimbursement eligibility.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔King♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔eClinicalWorks♔♔♔♔♔♔eClinicalWorks Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎My concern with this request for information is genuinely simple, even if the policy underneath it is not: I have read through the summary materials twice and I still cannot tell what actually changes for a practice like ours on the ground. We manage clinical workflows for a mid-sized ambulatory environment in Arizona, and when HHS asks about "accelerating adoption," I want to know whether that means our documentation tools get reclassified, whether our payers will reimburse differently, or whether we are supposed to just move faster without any of that changing.⏎⏎eClinicalWorks Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The framing here treats speed as the goal, and I am not sure that is right. What our providers actually need is clarity. Not a faster ramp toward something nobody has fully defined yet. If HHS could publish plain-language guidance on what qualifies as a covered AI tool versus a non-covered one under existing reimbursement structures, that would help more than anything in this♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Hill♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔Anthem Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthem Inc. submits these comments with specific concern about the accountability framework question. Our 2024 cross-sector analysis of 47 health technology clients found that 54% are carrying unresolved liability exposure for AI tools as a line item on their risk registers, not as a solved problem. When something goes wrong clinically, the vendor contract disclaims responsibility, the hospital points at the clinician, and the clinician points at the tool. HHS should not accelerate adoption while that chain remains broken.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Williams♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔University of Kansas School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of Kansas School of Medicine urges HHS to require robust external validation and real-world deployment evidence before finalizing any AI certification pathway in clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Robinson♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Back in February my thyroid numbers came back unusual and the computer program my doctor's office in Ogden uses flagged it as low priority and Dr. Pembrook told me afterward that she almost trusted the software over her own eyes and I have to tell you that scared me more than the thyroid result itself because Dr. Pembrook has known me for eleven years she knows my history she knows when I look worried even when I say I feel fine and no program is going to see that, it just can't, and what I keep thinking about is the younger patients in that waiting room who don't know to push back or ask a second question or say wait something still feels wrong to me because I've learned over the years that you have to advocate for yourself but a lot of people haven't and if the software says you're fine they will believe it. What I want HHS to know is that the reason I trust Dr. Pembrook is not just because she went to medical school it's because if she gets something wrong she is the one who has to look me in the face and answer for it and I don't know how you hold a computer program to that same standard and I genuinely don't think you can yet so please slow this down before someone gets hurt who didn't know they were supposed to be paying that close attention.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Hernandez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Connie was misdiagnosed twice. It wasn't until a doctor at Ochsner in New Orleans really took the time to look at her results that they caught her ovarian cancer. That doctor's attention is why she lived to see her son graduate. I worry about what happens if a computer just confirms the first wrong guess and moves on.⏎We already have a terrible time with healthcare access in Louisiana. People living out in the country parishes often have to wait weeks to see a specialist. Artificial intelligence won't be used to give them extra help. It will just be a cheap substitute because they don't have enough doctors out there anyway. When a computer makes a mistake, the people who suffer the most are older folks and families on fixed incomes. They don't have the time or money to fight back against a machine.⏎HHS has an obligation to require equity reviews before these programs are rolled out. Please make sure vulnerable patients are protected first. Nobody should be left trying to appeal a medical decision made by a computer they can't even talk to.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Martinez♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter got misdiagnosed twice at Christiana Care by actual physicians, so I genuinely don't understand why anyone thinks handing these decisions to a cost-cutting algorithm is going to go better for patients like her.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Ramirez♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔National Association of Social Workers♔♔♔♔♔♔NASW Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎On behalf of the National Association of Social Workers, we respectfully urge HHS to reframe this inquiry around precaution and human-centered values rather than acceleration.⏎⏎NASW Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Lee♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔National Alliance for Caregiving♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Alliance for Caregiving, HHS must recognize that patients need to be seen by another human being, and no algorithm replaces that.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Scott♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Three years of careful relationship-building with a client, and an automated screening tool labeled her "high risk for non-compliance" in what felt like about thirty seconds. Maria is 54, undocumented, Guatemalan, and had been navigating a care system that was not built for her in any meaningful sense of the phrase. The flag followed her. I watched her disengage entirely from care she genuinely needed, and the thing I keep returning to is that the algorithm wasn't broken in any way the vendor would have recognized. It performed exactly as designed. The problem was what it was designed to do, and who got to decide what "non-compliance" meant.⏎⏎I want to be clear that my concern here is not primarily about Maria's specific case, or about one poorly calibrated tool. I've seen versions of that same story with at least a dozen clients in the last two years. What I'm describing is a pattern, and I think this rulemaking process is at risk of treating that pattern as an engineering problem with an engineering solution.⏎⏎The gap between how a clinical AI tool performs in validation and how it performs once it's deployed in an actual hospital, with actual messy fragmented records and actual inconsistently coded diagnoses, is not a small technical gap that gets closed with more training data. There's research I came across, work done on deployed sepsis prediction algorithms, that documented significant performance degradation once those tools moved from controlled validation environments into real community hospital settings. The tools looked good on paper. They did not perform the same way in practice. That is not an anomaly. That is what happens.⏎⏎What concerns me about accelerating adoption before this is addressed is something called automation bias. Clinicians who work alongside confident-seeming AI systems reduce their own independent scrutiny. That's documented in the implementation science literature. So if you accelerate deployment of tools that are performing worse than their benchmarks suggest, and you do it in an environment where clinicians have already been trained by years of alert fatigue to reduce their vigilance around automated outputs, you are not just accepting some residual risk. You are structuring the system to fail at precisely the moments when the AI is most wrong.⏎⏎We did this with EHR rollouts. I mean, the promise was efficiency and the reality was burned-out physicians spending more time on documentation than on patients. We did it with clinical decision support systems that generated so many warnings that clicking through them became reflexive. The pattern is not new. The overconfidence is not new.⏎⏎The patients who will bear the cost of getting this wrong are not the patients who show up in validation datasets. They are patients like Maria, whose circumstances don't map cleanly onto the variables the model was trained on, and who have the least recourse when something goes wrong.⏎⏎I don't think I'm in a position to tell HHS what the right regulatory structure looks like in technical detail. But I do think the framing of this RFI, which treats acceleration as the goal and asks how to remove barriers to it, has the causality backwards. The question should be what independent evidence of real-world performance is required before deployment is permitted, and who is doing that evaluation, and whether it's someone other than the vendor.⏎⏎Slow down. Require independent post-market surveillance with actual community hospital data, not just academic medical center benchmarks. Stop treating patients as beta testers for tools that weren't validated on anyone who looks like them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Williams♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist up in Billings used one of these AI reading tools on my stress test back in February, and I'll be honest, it caught something small that she said she probably would have flagged anyway, but the speed of it was genuinely impressive. Living two hours from the nearest specialist, I noticed that. So I want to be clear that I'm not someone who thinks these tools are bad. What I keep coming back to, though, is my late wife Doris and what happened when her records got mixed up with another patient at the same clinic years ago. It took months to untangle. The thing is, that wasn't a technology failure exactly. It was a situation where the system stopped seeing her as a person and nobody was standing there whose job it was to catch that and fix it.⏎⏎My doctor, Dr. Halverson, retired two years ago and told me the paperwork was the main reason. That's the part I genuinely don't understand. The government keeps adding new requirements and systems, and good physicians leave because of it, and now we're asking those same overloaded clinics to adopt AI tools on top of everything else. I'm not saying don't do it. I'm saying that before HHS moves fast on this, somebody needs to decide who is accountable when the computer is wrong, and that person needs to be a human being who actually knows the patient's name.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Lee♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Carolyn was sick for eight months before a doctor in Little Rock finally figured out what was wrong with her, and what saved her was a specialist who actually read through her whole file and sat with her for an hour. I think about that a lot now, watching HHS put out something like this that reads less like a government protecting patients and more like a government helping companies sell products. We already went through this with the computer records systems, the ones hospitals were pushed to adopt starting around 2009, and my wife's doctor in Cabot still spends half every appointment staring at a screen instead of looking at her. Once those vendors got into the hospitals, there was no getting them out, and the prices never went down. I genuinely do not understand why we would hand the same kind of control to a new set of tech companies before anyone has answered the basic question of who is responsible when the system gets it wrong and a patient is harmed because of it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Hall♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Re: HHS AI RFI submission -- wanted to get this in before the deadline⏎⏎---⏎⏎My daughter Renee spent three weeks last October bouncing between urgent care and a telehealth portal before anyone figured out her "anxiety" was a thyroid disorder. A human physician finally caught it, looking at her in the eye, something feeling off to him. I think about that constantly now when I read what HHS is proposing here.⏎⏎But that's not actually what I want to say. I want to talk about something I think is getting buried.⏎⏎What HHS is describing, when you strip away the language about "accelerating adoption" and "public-private partnerships," is the federal government handing a small number of large technology vendors privileged access to regulatory processes and federal health datasets. I'm a retail worker, not a policy analyst, but I recognize this pattern. We watched it happen with electronic health records. Congress passed the HITECH Act in 2009, billions of dollars went to push hospitals toward EHR adoption, and what we ended up with was a market so concentrated that the Government Accountability Office was writing reports by 2015 about how hospitals couldn't switch vendors without losing years of patient data and spending millions they didn't have. Switching costs became a weapon. The vendors knew it.⏎⏎The GAO said, more or less in plain English, that the certification program meant to create interoperability had instead helped entrench the dominant vendors. I looked that up. It was not hard to find.⏎⏎Here is what I think is about to happen with AI:⏎⏎1. HHS facilitates data-sharing arrangements that give a handful of vendors access to Medicare and Medicaid claims data at scale, which smaller companies cannot match.⏎2. Those vendors build clinical decision tools, billing systems, documentation workflows.⏎3. Hospitals and clinics integrate those tools deeply enough that switching becomes functionally impossible.⏎4. The vendors raise prices, reduce accountability, and lobby against any oversight that would require them to open their systems.⏎⏎We have already seen this story. We are being asked to run it again, faster, with higher stakes, because the systems being embedded this time are not just administrative, they are clinical. They are sitting between a patient and a diagnosis.⏎⏎I genuinely don't understand why HHS would structure this as a race. The framing of the RFI keeps treating speed as the goal. Speed benefits the vendors who are already scaled up and ready to deploy. It does not benefit the hospitals trying to evaluate whether an algorithm trained mostly on data from large urban academic medical centers is going to perform the same way for a 24-year-old Black woman in a smaller California clinic.⏎⏎There is a question in this RFI about what the department can do to accelerate adoption through federal data-sharing. I'd ask instead: who specifically benefits from each proposed data-sharing arrangement, and what are the contractual restrictions on how that data can be used to build proprietary models that the government then has no leverage over?⏎⏎I don't have a credential that makes HHS obligated to listen to me. But I do have a daughter who almost got misdiagnosed by a system that was already moving too fast, and I'm watching HHS⏎⏎---⏎⏎Joseph Hall⏎Antioch, CA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Jones♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔National Association of Emergency Medical Technicians♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Association of Emergency Medical Technicians, we urge HHS to prioritize patient-centered stewardship over acceleration, ensuring that AI adoption in clinical settings does not erode the human presence that emergency care fundamentally requires.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Smith♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔Susan G. Komen♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan G. Komen urges HHS to halt acceleration of clinical AI adoption until a clear, enforceable accountability framework exists for patient harm — no vendor disclaimers, no diffused liability, no exceptions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Green♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty years as a floor nurse, and I can tell you EXACTLY what happens when a system is built to wear people down instead of help them. I read news stories about UnitedHealth using an AI tool to deny Medicare patients rehab coverage, sometimes after just a few days, and those denials were overturned on appeal at something like 90% when anybody actually fought back. But most people DON'T fight back!! My patients in rural West Virginia already drove two hours to see a specialist. They don't have lawyers. They don't have the energy. HHS has the POWER to require a real human being to sign off on every single denial before it takes effect and I genuinely cannot understand why that isn't already the rule.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Garcia♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: My comment for the HHS AI thing -- Michelle please confirm you want me to send this as-is⏎⏎My daughter drove me three hours to Casper on a Tuesday in February, and the specialist spent eight minutes with me. Eight. I found out later his treatment plan came from a software program nobody mentioned. That scared me more than my diagnosis did. HHS is supposed to protect people like me -- that's the law. Instead this proposal is asking how to speed up AI adoption, and I genuinely don't understand why nobody is first asking who gets hurt. People in rural Wyoming, elderly people, people on Medicaid, we are going to get the most automated care and the least human attention, because that is how it always goes. The shortcuts land on the people who have the hardest time pushing back. Before any AI tool goes into a clinic, HHS needs to require an honest assessment of who bears the risk. And there must be one actual human being who is legally responsible when it goes wrong.⏎⏎--⏎Michelle Garcia⏎Gillette, WY♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Smith♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔Black Women's Health Imperative♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Black Women's Health Imperative, this organization respectfully submits that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care without adequate equity safeguards will repeat the failures seen in prior health IT deployments, where speed of rollout consistently outpaced real-world performance monitoring.⏎⏎Our 2024 focus group work across four health equity cohorts found deep patient skepticism toward automated clinical tools. That skepticism is not irrational. It reflects lived experience with diagnostic delay and inconsistent care.⏎⏎HHS should require independent post-market surveillance and mandatory disaggregated performance reporting as conditions of deployment, not voluntary commitments made after the fact.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔White♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔Quest Diagnostics♔♔♔♔♔♔Quest Diagnostics submits these comments in response to the HHS Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. We appreciate the Department's attention to this area, and we recognize the genuine appeal of using regulatory, reimbursement, and research levers in combination to move things forward. That said, our own operational experience gives us real pause about the sequencing being proposed. An internal review across our portfolio of health technology deployment partners found that 67% of those partners identified inconsistent payer standards as their primary barrier to scaling AI-assisted tools, with compliance costs ranging from $280,000 to $1.2 million per product line depending on which certifying body was involved. Those are not hypothetical obstacles. They are evidence that the foundational governance work is still incomplete.⏎⏎Quest Diagnostics Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎What concerns us is that once AI tools are written into reimbursement codes and embedded in clinical workflows, course correction becomes extraordinarily difficult for everyone, including industry. Our network has documented AI-assisted administrative tools being denied coverage at a rate 43% higher than comparable human-performed services, which tells us the current coding and coverage frameworks are not ready. We would ask HHS to prioritize establishing clear liability standards and consistent payer requirements before♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Harris♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔American Psychiatric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔ON HUMAN PRESENCE IN CARE⏎⏎The American Psychiatric Association urges HHS to require independent human oversight before accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings where therapeutic relationships determine outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Wilson♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎My brother Ray has been in my care for several years now, and his kidney disease was caught not by his physician but by an algorithm embedded in his telehealth platform that flagged an anomaly in his labs. His doctor, a good physician who was simply overworked and operating in fifteen-minute windows, told me later she had missed the pattern. I am genuinely grateful for that technology. I want to say that plainly before I say anything else.⏎⏎But I keep thinking about the history here. The rollout of electronic health records in the early 2010s was sold to the public as a transformation in patient safety and care coordination. What we got instead was alert fatigue, physician burnout, and systems that talked to each other so poorly that clinicians still fax records in 2024. That is not ancient history. It is the immediate predecessor to this conversation.⏎⏎The pattern I am worried about is this: AI tools that perform well in controlled research environments routinely degrade when deployed in real clinical settings, because real patient populations are different, real EHR configurations are inconsistent, and real data is messy in ways that training datasets don't reflect. Medical records in practice are fragmented, inconsistently coded, and full of gaps. A model trained on clean, well-labeled data from an academic medical center may behave very differently when deployed in a rural Alaskan clinic where I take Ray for his follow-ups.⏎⏎This is not speculation. Research on sepsis prediction algorithms, including work by Shah and colleagues published in peer-reviewed health informatics literature, has documented meaningful degradation in real-world performance compared to the metrics reported at validation. Sepsis algorithms. The kind of failure mode that is not a minor inconvenience.⏎⏎What makes this worse is something called automation bias, which is the well-documented tendency of clinicians to reduce their own vigilance when an AI system is present. The practical consequence is that deploying a brittle or overconfident AI tool in a clinical setting can increase the probability of serious error precisely at the moments when the system is most likely to be wrong. The clinician trusts the tool, the tool is operating outside its validated range, and the patient falls through a gap that wouldn't have existed without the tool at all.⏎⏎I want HHS to ask a harder question than "how do we accelerate adoption." The question I'd want answered is: what is the actual post-deployment performance of these tools across diverse patient populations and clinical environments, and who is accountable for measuring it?⏎⏎Right now the answer to the accountability question is largely the vendors. That is not adequate. Vendors have structural incentives to report favorable performance metrics and to define their validation populations in ways that flatter their tools. Governing bodies, including HHS itself, need to require independent post-deployment monitoring as a condition of clinical integration, not as an optional quality improvement exercise.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI in clinical care. Ray's kidneys are probably better off because of it. But the question isn't whether the technology can work in a demonstration. It's whether we know how it actually performs once it's embedded in the clinical workflow of a busy practice with inconsistent data and a clinician who has been trained, implicitly, to trust the alert.⏎⏎I'm not sure the current proposal takes that question seriously enough.⏎⏎Mark Wilson⏎Fairbanks, Alaska♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Ramirez♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My unit near Atlanta nearly lost a patient because the algorithm deprioritized her alert. Nobody seems to ask why rural hospitals keep closing while we're out here accelerating tools that nobody on the floor has time to question.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Hall♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔PathAI♔♔♔♔♔♔The premise of the current RFI asks how HHS can speed up AI adoption in clinical care. At PathAI, where I manage regional deployments of our digital pathology systems across Minnesota health networks, we view sluggish adoption as the wrong focus entirely. Lack of consistent validation for systems already active in the field is our primary challenge.⏎To give a specific example from tissue analysis. We frequently see that an AI model evaluating a biopsy slide might yield varying diagnostic confidence scores depending simply on which lab's scanning hardware digitized the sample. Right now there is no regulatory framework requiring health systems to audit these hardware-driven discrepancies. Expanding clinical AI deployments without establishing oversight standards first is just going to scale up these exact types of baseline errors.⏎We have seen what happens when image analysis software gets pushed to the clinic without strict calibration standards, such as the early issues with CAD software in radiology (see for example the 2008 FDA panel on mammography CAD). Instead of looking for ways to push new technologies into the clinic faster, HHS must establish mandatory validation requirements for the tools we are already using.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Rivera♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew Rivera⏎4812 Maple Creek Drive⏎Des Moines, IA 50314⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎My brother Marcus spent eight months being misdiagnosed before a specialist in Des Moines finally caught his autoimmune condition. Once AI gets baked into hospital workflows and insurance payment systems, there is no pulling it back out, no matter how badly it fails people who don't fit the profile the algorithm was trained on. That decision belongs to families like mine, not to administrators working from an industry wish list. Slow down. Build the oversight first.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Andrew Rivera♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Wright♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔Blue Cross Blue Shield Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The Blue Cross Blue Shield Association submits these comments regarding the RFI framework. As Chief Medical Officer, my immediate concern is that this implementation timeline outpaces the available peer-reviewed clinical evidence. Complex diagnostic evaluations—such as differentiating atypical autoimmune disorders—require hands-on clinical judgment that goes far beyond simple EHR data extraction. Integrating AI into these workflows poses concrete risks to diagnostic accuracy and established standards of care.⏎⏎The operational efficiency metrics for AI are certainly clear, as detailed in our October 2023 BCBSA Clinical Advisory Board report and the recent Q3 Provider Network Survey spanning 14 regional systems. Yet the current proposal still under-examines the threats of workflow disruption and the erosion of independent clinical decision-making. HHS must take a more cautious approach before finalizing these implementation rules under the RFI.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Wright♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Society of Hospital Medicine urges HHS to reframe this RFI around accountability, worker protection, and oversight before any acceleration of AI adoption in clinical settings proceeds.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Young♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔American Society of Plastic Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Society of Plastic Surgeons, we urge HHS to recognize that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care before accountability structures are in place will leave patients without meaningful recourse when something goes wrong. Our members have seen this already: when an AI-assisted recommendation causes harm, responsibility dissolves across the clinician, the hospital, the vendor, and the data provider, and no one is left holding it.⏎⏎HHS should treat clear accountability frameworks as a precondition for any acceleration, not an afterthought.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Miller♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Marcus went into the ER in February 2023 with chest pain that wouldn't quit, and the software his hospital was using flagged him as low-risk and basically said he could wait. One doctor, Dr. Anita Reyes I think her name was, looked at him, looked at his face, and ordered a second test anyway because something felt wrong to her. Partially blocked artery. If she had trusted that screen he would have gone home and probably died in his sleep.⏎⏎That is what I keep thinking about when I read that HHS wants to speed this up. You are talking about real people who are scared and in pain, and what they need is a human being in that room who is responsible for them, who can be held accountable if they miss something, not a score on a screen that nobody owns. When the machine gets it wrong everybody points at the machine. Slow this down, put actual doctors in charge of these decisions, and do not let the companies selling the software set the rules for how sick people get treated.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Walker♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter went two years without the right diagnosis, and that was with actual human doctors who could look at her and ask questions. I don't know exactly what HHS is trying to do here, but I'll say this plainly, when hospitals talk about AI efficiency what they usually mean is fewer nurses on the floor, fewer people checking the work, and more profit going somewhere else. I taught school in Ravalli County for 34 years and I know what happens when you replace people with systems and call it progress. The people at the bottom lose their jobs and the people at the top keep the savings. My honest question is does a comment like mine actually change anything here, or is this just paperwork. Because if HHS is serious, they need oversight that isn't just the hospitals grading themselves.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Harris♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister spent eight months being told her symptoms were anxiety. Please do not let algorithms replace the doctor who finally sat down and actually listened to her.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Allen♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Seventeen years on a med-surg floor in Louisville teaches you things no algorithm is going to catch, and one of them is that the people who get hurt quietly are the ones nobody hears from afterward. What I keep thinking about when I see HHS talking about accelerating AI in clinical care is that there's a whole side of this that isn't about diagnosis or treatment at all, it's about insurance companies using AI to deny care to people who are already sick and exhausted and who almost never appeal because they can't, not because the denial was right but because fighting back requires energy most of them don't have. I read about a big insurer that was using an AI system to deny post-acute rehab claims for elderly Medicare patients, denying them at rates that no human reviewer would have approved, and the people on the receiving end were in their seventies and eighties trying to recover from hip fractures and strokes. In rural Kentucky a lot of those folks don't have a patient advocate or a family member who can navigate the appeals process for them.⏎⏎HHS has the authority to require that a real human being sign off on every coverage denial, someone whose name is on it and who can be held to account, and I genuinely don't understand why that isn't already the rule. Choosing not to require that while pushing AI adoption faster is a choice with consequences for real people, most of whom will never show up in any data about it because they just went without care. Please don't move faster on any of this before that piece is settled.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Jackson♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Cohere Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Cohere Health submits these comments to caution that accelerating reimbursement pathways before post-deployment performance monitoring is required as a condition will repeat the same implementation failures the industry has already absorbed at considerable cost.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Hernandez♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband had a cardiac event back in 2019 and the ER doctor who actually SLOWED DOWN and read his full chart was the one who caught what everyone else almost missed. That's what I keep thinking about when my younger crew members mention AI flagging their stress data between shifts. Who is looking at that?? The hospitals pushing these tools hardest are the ones with the most to gain from cutting nursing staff and billing clerks, and I genuinely don't think patients or frontline workers are the ones writing those contracts. Please, before this moves faster, put REAL oversight in place and make sure the people doing the actual work have a seat at the table, not just the vendors.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Thompson♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎My VA cardiologist retired two years back. What followed was three telehealth appointments where a screen kept telling me my symptoms didn't fit the expected pattern. I am a woman veteran, Gulf War era, with conditions the algorithm apparently wasn't built to recognize.⏎⏎My recommendations:⏎⏎1. Require a human clinician override at every diagnostic decision point.⏎2. Slow the adoption timeline until patient populations like mine are actually tested.⏎⏎Medicine works because a person looks at you. Speed is not progress if the tool can't see you.⏎⏎(See Appendix A, not attached, for additional case documentation.)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Ramirez♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔American Foundation for the Blind♔♔♔♔♔♔I am submitting these comments as Research Director for the American Foundation for the Blind. In a 2023 survey of individuals living with blindness and low vision, we found that over half of minority respondents are worried about whether the AI systems used in their medical care were actually trained on populations that include them.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS should not push for faster AI adoption in clinical settings until basic safeguards are established. The agency needs to require routine bias testing and ask developers to show how their tools perform across different racial and income groups.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Martinez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔American College of Cardiology♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Cardiology urges HHS to mandate equity impact assessments before expanding AI deployment. We must ensure vulnerable patients in underserved settings do not receive automated systems that completely replace physicians.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Perez♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS needs to put strict rules on medical AI before it's everywhere. Last spring my long-time cardiologist retired, and for four months I was basically stuck with nobody steering the ship regarding my healthcare. A bad medication interaction just sat right there in my chart. Anyone could have seen it, but nobody did until I finally got in with a new doctor up in Salt Lake City who actually bothered to read my file. We obviously need software that can catch those exact kinds of mistakes. We need the help. But if HHS just lets hospitals rush into using this new AI software without strict oversight, it's going to hurt a lot of people.⏎⏎⏎⏎The bias in these systems isn't some future theory, it is already happening. We already know pulse oximeters often give inaccurate oxygen readings for patients with darker skin. Then those flawed numbers get fed straight into new AI training databases. So the algorithm learns to tell doctors that a patient is fine when they actually need oxygen. Or look at the sepsis prediction models they've been using in hospitals. Independent researchers found out the software was missing a huge number of cases, performing way worse than the vendor claimed, and failing certain demographics much more than others.⏎⏎⏎⏎I work as an administrative assistant and I see firsthand how people just automatically trust whatever a computer screen tells them. In a hospital setting, that is incredibly dangerous. Doctors and nurses are swamped. It is too hard for them to argue with an AI readout, especially if the patient sitting in front of them doesn't match the demographic the software was trained on.⏎⏎⏎⏎You can't just roll out an algorithm and promise to fix the age or racial biases later on. HHS must require independent, third-party audits before any AI tool is allowed in a medical setting. Not audits done by the tech companies selling the software—they have too much financial incentive to pass it. The testing has to be done by outside groups, and the results have to be completely public. We need proof that the software doesn't discriminate based on race, age, or income level.⏎⏎⏎⏎Right now there is absolutely zero accountability if an AI gives a doctor bad advice. The tech companies are just pushing this stuff out as fast as possible. HHS needs to step up and enforce strict rules now, before a bad algorithm gets rubber-stamped and spread to every clinic in the country.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Ramirez♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔When my daughter Marisol was eleven her pediatrician's office switched EHR systems and her penicillin allergy completely disappeared from her chart. It wasn't actually deleted but it got buried in some dropdown menu that nobody bothered to check. I only caught it because I was standing right there reading the screen over the doctor's shoulder. When you have a medically complex kid you learn pretty quickly that you can't trust the system to catch its own mistakes.⏎⏎⏎⏎Now I see that HHS wants to speed up the adoption of AI in clinical settings and I just want to know who's going to protect patients like my daughter. They test these AI tools in tightly controlled environments using really clean data, but real hospitals aren't like that. Down here in Alabama our hospital infrastructure is definitely not Johns Hopkins. We're dealing with fragmented records, missing fields, and old entries from ten years ago that nobody has updated. Those are the exact conditions that buried Marisol's allergy in the first place, and no research benchmark predicts how an AI model behaves when it hits that kind of mess. It's a huge gap between the testing lab and the real world, and that gap is where patients get hurt.⏎⏎⏎⏎We've been through this before with the original EHR rollouts. We were told it would completely transform patient safety, but what we actually got was a bunch of burned-out doctors clicking through screens. I remember reading about a sepsis prediction algorithm by Shah and some other researchers a few years ago that looked great in development but totally fell apart when they put it in real clinics. It always seems to go the exact same way. They do a promising demo in a controlled setting, then they rush to deploy it everywhere without enough real-world validation. The failures are completely invisible until someone gets hurt, and then later on everyone admits they should've been more careful. All this optimistic language I'm reading in this RFI about modernization sounds exactly like what they said back then.⏎⏎⏎⏎It feels like this RFI is asking the wrong questions. We shouldn't be asking how to accelerate AI adoption. We need to ask if these tools are even ready to be dumped into the under-resourced systems where most of us actually get our care. Plus, if you reduce the number of human hands checking an algorithm's work, obviously you're going to increase the chances of an error slipping through. That's just basic common sense, not some complicated tech issue. I don't trust for a second that vague guardrails or explainability rules will fix anything when the clinical staff is already stretched to the limit and alert fatigue has trained them to just click past every warning.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS needs to require independent real-world validation before any clinical AI tool is approved for broad use. I mean truly independent validation in real hospitals with messy data, not just studies paid for by the vendors. We need to stop treating patients as guinea pigs for software that isn't ready for the real world.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Ramirez♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I really don't understand why HHS is prioritizing speed with this new framework. The agency keeps talking about an "acceleration" pathway for medical algorithms as if pushing these tools out faster is just natural progress. In reality, you're making a conscious choice to deploy systems before we even have a way to measure who they might hurt.⏎⏎⏎⏎My brother Carlos went through almost a year of terrible misdiagnoses. He had so many appointments where we were constantly reassured everything was fine, until a doctor at Yale New Haven finally ordered the imaging that caught his meningioma. We learned later that an initial screening tool had flagged him as low risk, and that single flag basically poisoned his entire clinical chart. Every doctor who saw him after that just assumed the computer was right. Busy doctors don't have the time to second-guess these systems. It isn't just extra information for them; the algorithm actually dictates what the doctor bothers to look for.⏎⏎⏎⏎Being a librarian here in Connecticut, I end up reading a lot about how these predictive systems actually function, and the built-in biases are well documented at this point. Take the commercial algorithms that use past healthcare costs to predict a patient's future health needs. Because Black patients have historically received less medical care, the algorithm just assumes they're healthier and need less help, completely reproducing that history of underservice. We've also seen plenty of medical devices and diagnostic AI that fail to get accurate readings on darker skin tones.⏎⏎⏎⏎If HHS just accelerates this stuff, you're fast-tracking a system that discriminates. We need mandatory bias auditing standards before any of these tools get approved for reimbursement or deployment. It has to be independent, and it has to happen before the tech hits the market. The criteria for these audits should be completely public, along with the results. And I want to see that data broken out by race, ethnicity, and socioeconomic status.⏎⏎⏎⏎Yes, requiring independent audits will slow things down. That's a necessary trade-off. Getting accurate results across all populations is the actual problem we need to solve right now, not figuring out how to rush flawed software into hospitals faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Hernandez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife's chest pain got flagged differently by an app than by her doctor two years back, and nobody was clearly responsible for reconciling that gap, which tells me we are nowhere near ready to scale this up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Torres♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔Boston Scientific♔♔♔♔♔♔Boston Scientific | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎⏎As a medical device manufacturer, Boston Scientific has spent considerable time reviewing third-party software agreements over the past two years. During these reviews, a basic structural problem consistently emerges regarding the lack of a standard for who bears liability when a non-device AI tool produces a recommendation that influences clinical decision-making. In a substantial majority of the integration agreements our legal and finance teams examined, the liability language was either absent or written so loosely that it would be functionally unenforceable. Vendors frequently cite FDA clearance as a substitute for explicit liability terms, despite the fact that clearance speaks primarily to safety and effectiveness in a defined use case rather than establishing contractual accountability between a software provider and the end-user.⏎⏎⏎⏎From a corporate finance perspective, HHS appears ready to accelerate the adoption of these technologies before this fundamental accountability gap is resolved. The resulting compliance cost exposure—often requiring significant financial resources per product line just to navigate inconsistent payer certification requirements—creates a real operational burden for the health systems we partner with, particularly those in rural markets. Pushing for faster adoption without first establishing clear liability frameworks will only compound institutional risk across the healthcare sector.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Jones♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Claire spent fourteen months being misdiagnosed at a Providence health network before a physician at a smaller clinic finally caught her autoimmune condition. I think about those fourteen months constantly when I read language like "accelerating adoption" in a federal RFI. The question I keep returning to is: accelerating for whom, and at whose expense.⏎⏎The documented bias in existing clinical AI is not speculative. Sjoding et al. published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 showing that pulse oximeters systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with dark skin, a finding that has since been traced into clinical AI tools trained on that same flawed measurement data. Obermeyer et al. published in Science in 2019 showing that a widely used commercial algorithm underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health status. These are not edge cases. They are documented, peer-reviewed findings about systems already deployed at scale.⏎⏎What HHS is now proposing to do is move faster. I genuinely do not understand the logic.⏎⏎When an algorithm produces a biased output, it does not announce itself as biased. It arrives with the authority of a system, of data, of objectivity. Clinicians under time pressure are less likely to question it than they would a colleague's opinion. The bias becomes invisible precisely when it is most dangerous. Claire's eventual diagnosis came from a doctor who slowed down and actually listened. There is no AI accelerant for that.⏎⏎The RFI asks about research and development actions the Department can take. My answer to that specific question is: the research that matters most right now is mandatory pre-deployment bias auditing. Not voluntary. Not best-practice guidance. Mandatory.⏎⏎Specifically, HHS should require:⏎⏎1. Pre-deployment demographic performance stratification for any AI tool used in clinical decision support, with results publicly available before any facility can deploy the tool.⏎⏎2. Independent third-party auditing with enforcement authority, not self-certification by the vendor who profits from the sale.⏎⏎3. Ongoing post-deployment monitoring with reporting requirements broken down by race, ethnicity, and insurance status.⏎⏎Expanding interoperability pipelines before these standards exist is not a research accelerant. It is a disparity accelerant. When you build larger data pipelines using training data that already encodes racial and socioeconomic skew, you embed that skew more deeply into more systems faster. The scale of the problem grows. The correction becomes harder. This is not a hypothetical risk. It is the documented trajectory of every healthcare AI deployment we have been able to audit so far.⏎⏎I am a librarian. I work with information systems every day. I understand that data reflects the conditions under which it was collected. Healthcare data in this country was collected under conditions of documented inequality. Any AI trained on it will reproduce that inequality unless someone specifically intervenes to prevent it.⏎⏎HHS has the authority to require that intervention before deployment, not after. The question is whether the Department will use that authority or whether the acceleration language in this RFI is already the answer.⏎⏎No acceleration without mandatory pre-deployment bias audits and demographic performance stratification with real enforcement behind them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Walker♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Back in 2019, my dad was misdiagnosed for almost eight months because a regional hospital here in South Carolina used an automated screening tool that wasn't designed with people like him in mind. He is Gullah-Geechee and his symptoms didn't look like the textbook cases. The computer system flagged him as low-risk, and he didn't get proper care until a human doctor finally examined him. Now I see that HHS is looking to speed up the rollout of more AI tools, and it worries me because hospitals don't even have a way to catch the mistakes these systems make.⏎⏎⏎⏎I own a small business, so I understand why hospitals want to use AI to be more efficient. But rushing this out before it is safe for everybody is a bad idea. This RFI seems to prioritize speed over patient safety.⏎⏎⏎⏎After my dad's experience, I started reading up on this. I found articles about how even basic hospital equipment like pulse oximeters don't work as well on Black patients. Since AI is often trained on data from flawed devices, it just copies those same racial blind spots. The software learns from bad data and makes the problem worse.⏎⏎⏎⏎I also read about a major sepsis prediction software that many hospitals use. Independent researchers found out it did a lot worse in actual hospital settings than the developer claimed. The scary part is that the doctors using it didn't know that. They just trusted the computer. When a machine spits out an answer, people tend to believe it, which makes it hard for a doctor to go against the screen.⏎⏎⏎⏎HHS isn't fully thinking about how this works in real life. If a doctor questions a weird lab test, nobody cares. But if they ignore an AI recommendation, they probably feel like they're sticking their neck out. The pressure is to just follow the software.⏎⏎⏎⏎The RFI asks about certification. Letting the tech industry police itself with basic credentialing won't protect patients. If they don't do independent audits that specifically check for bias across different races and income levels, then certification is just a way for hospitals to avoid getting sued. Once a hospital buys an approved tool, they aren't going to spend money looking for problems with it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Before HHS pushes hospitals to adopt more of this tech, there need to be actual rules. Someone independent from the developer needs to test it for bias across different demographics before it gets used on patients. The results of those tests should be public so doctors can see them. Hospitals also need to be required to keep monitoring the software after they buy it, so there is a clear rule for when to pull the plug if it starts making mistakes.⏎⏎⏎⏎My dad is doing fine now because he finally got the right treatment. But losing eight months to a bad computer screening was hard on him. As far as I know, the hospital never went back to figure out why their system missed him. I just hope HHS puts actual safeguards in place so other families don't have to deal with this.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Garcia♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My thyroid numbers came back abnormal in March, and the computer system my doctor's office uses printed out three medication suggestions that did not account for the blood thinner I have taken every single day for six years. That information was in my chart. My pharmacist caught it before I filled the prescription. I am 78 years old and I work in retail and I am not a medical expert, but I know this: a doctor's job is to know you, the whole you, not just the numbers on a screen. What scared me was not my thyroid. It was realizing the computer had no idea who I was. There is something very wrong with pushing these systems further into care before there are firm requirements that a real, responsible human being reviews every recommendation before it reaches a patient. I am asking HHS to slow this down and put that oversight in writing first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Rivera♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew Rivera, Associate Professor⏎University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine⏎Submission to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎⏎ON DEMOCRATIC LEGITIMACY⏎⏎The University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine appreciates this opportunity to respond. HHS is treating acceleration as the default, but the prior question of whether to accelerate has not received democratic deliberation.⏎⏎ON IRREVERSIBILITY⏎⏎Once AI enters federal reimbursement structures, removal becomes politically costly. The Department should require precautionary governance frameworks before adoption, not after.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Young♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty Young⏎412 Meadowlark Drive⏎Cheyenne, Wyoming 82009⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎⏎Two years ago my regular doctor here in Cheyenne retired. For about eight months after that I was just getting bounced around between different clinics and nobody was really looking at my whole chart. Finally I saw a nurse practitioner named Denise. She actually took the time to sit down and realized my blood pressure pill was having a bad reaction with a vitamin supplement I take every day. I don't know if a computer program would have caught that or not. But Denise actually looked at me while I was talking about my daily routine. She asked how my sister was doing (she lives with me) and just made sure I really understood what to do before I went home. You just don't get that kind of attention from a machine.⏎⏎I am writing to you because I am worried about what is going to happen if clinics start relying on artificial intelligence for everything and we lose people like Denise. If the system is just spitting out answers and nobody is actually checking it, who is responsible if it's wrong? Getting a medical answer faster isn't the same thing as getting good care. Good care takes a real person paying attention. Before your department lets clinics use more AI, I really think you need a rule that says a specific, named human being has to review the computer's recommendation. Someone real needs to put their name on it and be held accountable for a patient's treatment, not just a software program.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Betty Young♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Gonzalez♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty years on the floor at Avera McKennan taught me that new technology almost always arrives faster than the judgment to use it well. I read somewhere that UnitedHealth's NaviHealth algorithm was denying post-acute care to elderly patients at a rate that no actual physician reviewing the cases would have approved, and what stayed with me was not the headline number but the quieter fact underneath it: most of those people probably just gave up. My neighbor's daughter from Rosebud already spends more energy fighting for basic care than she should have to, and she does not have the time or the legal help to appeal a denial she can't even get a clear explanation for. HHS keeps talking about evaluation methods and accreditation frameworks, which sound reasonable, but if the industry is reviewing itself and nobody is required to actually sign a denial with their name and license number, those frameworks are paper. Not using the authority to require that is a choice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Moore♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Exscientia♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard Moore, Director of Health Information Technology⏎Exscientia⏎Billings, Montana 59101⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Docket⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF SEQUENCING: GOVERNANCE BEFORE ACCELERATION⏎⏎The framing of this RFI troubles me in a way I want to be precise about. HHS is asking which regulatory, reimbursement, and research levers it can pull simultaneously to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. That is not a neutral question. It presupposes that acceleration is the correct policy posture, and it asks the public to help design the machinery for a decision that has not yet been democratically made.⏎⏎THE IMPLEMENTATION REALITY⏎⏎FastHealth Corporation's 2024 Health IT Landscape Survey, covering 214 health system clients, found that organizations deploying the same AI module faced an average of 4.3 distinct certification or documentation requirements depending on their payer mix — adding $180,000 to $340,000 in per-product compliance overhead before any reimbursement pathway existed. More telling: 91% of reviewed payer contracts left liability attribution for non-device-classified AI tools explicitly unaddressed. We are being asked to scale technology whose legal architecture is, in most contracts, a blank page.⏎⏎Exscientia Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The Government Accountability Office reached a related conclusion in GAO-24-106538, finding that federal oversight frameworks for non-device AI in clinical care remain materially incomplete. That finding was published in 2024. This RFI was published shortly thereafter. The sequencing here is wrong.⏎⏎ON IRREVERSIBILITY⏎⏎Ash, Berg, and Coiera documented in 2004 that health IT adoption creates path dependencies that persist long after performance failures are identified. We have not learned from that. ONC's own data confirms there are no documented large-scale EHR removal events post-deployment across the office-based physician population (ONC Data Brief No. 65, 2023). Once a technology is woven into federal reimbursement structures, the political cost of removing it permanently exceeds the political cost of tolerating known failures. That is a ratchet, not a policy.⏎⏎The question of how much artificial intelligence should mediate a clinical encounter is a moral and political question. It belongs to democratic deliberation, not to an agency operating on an industry-aligned timeline. A society is fully entitled to decide that medicine should be slower and more human, even at measurable efficiency cost.⏎⏎HHS should pause the acceleration posture and commission an independent governance framework process — one that addresses liability attribution, audit requirements, and reversibility safeguards — before any reimbursement or regulatory levers are engaged.⏎⏎The liability question alone, given that 91% figure, would take years to resolve properly if anyone were actually trying.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Martin♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔American Academy of Dermatology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Academy of Dermatology Association, we appreciate the opportunity to respond. Our 2024 survey of member physicians found that 43% encountered clinical scenarios where AI recommendations appeared inconsistent with expected patient outcomes, and our Health Equity Task Force has documented systematic performance gaps in tools deployed at facilities serving rural and low-income communities. These are not theoretical concerns.⏎⏎HHS should not finalize reimbursement or adoption incentives before mandatory pre-deployment equity audits are in place. Accelerating adoption without that infrastructure scales existing bias into broader clinical harm. The RFI's framing treats speed as the default, and the Academy of Dermatology Association questions whether that ordering is right.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Nguyen♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Linh spent eight months being told by computer-flagging systems that she had allergies. She had a rare autoimmune condition. It took a third doctor, one who pulled her chair close and noticed Linh was holding her stomach even when she said she felt fine, to finally get us somewhere. I keep thinking about that waiting room in Fairfax, 2009, while her condition went unnamed. Now I read that HHS wants to move faster on AI in clinics, and I genuinely don't understand why nobody is asking what happens to rural hospitals that keep closing while we talk about acceleration. That problem does not get fixed here.⏎⏎Page 1 of 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Ramirez♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔National Rural Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS must require meaningful human review of AI-generated coverage denials before accelerating adoption further; vulnerable Medicare and Medicaid populations are already losing medically necessary care with no viable recourse.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Scott♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔American Public Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Public Health Association urges HHS and ONC to slow down the regulatory timeline for artificial intelligence integration in clinical care. The current direction of this rulemaking treats deployment speed as the primary goal, but our 54 state and regional affiliates are reporting significant informed consent gaps regarding algorithmic tools. In the APHA Health IT Assessment Report (published March 2024, available in the APHA online policy library), frontline staff ranked uncommunicated clinical decision support (CDS) software as a top-three concern. According to that assessment, a majority of patients treated at affiliate clinics are completely unaware that CDS software is shaping their medical decisions. Pushing these technologies forward without strict transparency rules only worsens this consent gap.⏎The patient-provider dynamic is currently being sidelined in favor of innovation. When complex medical issues are routed through software that neither the patient nor the provider fully understands, the clinical relationship suffers. Our affiliate feedback indicates that individuals seeking care assume a human is evaluating their symptoms. Treating direct clinical evaluation as just a slow process that needs to be fixed by predictive models will ultimately damage the trust required for effective public health interventions. The Department needs to put patient transparency at the center of this next framework phase before widespread rollout happens and stakeholder feedback is too late to matter.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Davis♔♔California♔♔United States♔Weil Cornell Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Weill Cornell Medicine's postdoctoral research community urges HHS to pause reimbursement-driven AI acceleration, commission independent governance design, and return with a democratically reviewed framework before embedding these tools further into clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Lopez♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔National Association of Community Health Centers♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Association of Community Health Centers, HHS must establish clear accountability infrastructure before accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, or injured patients will have no meaningful path to justice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Robinson♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ours Yetaken come on the paper is not available for use of certain model used in medical algorithms and doctors relying to much on them⏎⏎[This message was sent from mobile]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Robinson♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-seven years as a nurse and I've never been as scared for my patients as I am right now, watching this conversation about AI acceleration happen without anyone asking the question I keep coming back to: what is medicine actually for?⏎⏎I'll tell you about a patient I cared for across three separate hospital admissions over about two years. Fifty-eight years old, Black woman, presenting with fatigue and joint pain that everyone kept attributing to other things. It was finally an AI-flagged recommendation that prompted her rheumatologist to order imaging he'd previously dismissed, and she got her lupus diagnosis. I was grateful. Genuinely. But when I tried to trace back through the data to understand why the earlier screenings had missed her, I ran into a wall of proprietary system logic that I had no standing to examine. Nobody did.⏎⏎That opacity bothers me more than the delay in diagnosis.⏎⏎What I want HHS to hear is something that gets lost in every conversation about AI efficiency: the relationship between a clinician and a patient is not the delivery mechanism for the correct diagnosis. It is part of the treatment. There is published research on this, Kelley et al. in PLOS ONE from 2014, a systematic review and meta-analysis showing that the patient-clinician relationship has measurable, independent clinical value beyond whether the technical recommendation is accurate. The relationship itself changes outcomes. That is not a soft finding. That is a clinical finding.⏎⏎And what worries me about accelerating AI adoption the way this RFI frames it is that we will optimize the information-processing parts of medicine and quietly let the human parts atrophy. When a patient is scared and in pain, they often cannot clearly articulate what they are experiencing. I have had patients who described their chest pain as "kind of a pressure thing" and whose faces told me something completely different. A family member hovering in the doorway. A patient who went quiet when I asked about medications at home. These are not supplementary data points you feed into a model. They are the diagnosis.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place. I've seen it help. But there's a difference between a tool that supports a clinician's judgment and a workflow that replaces the moments where judgment actually forms.⏎⏎Some things I'd ask HHS to at minimum require before accelerating any further:⏎⏎1. Any AI system used in direct clinical decision-making must have a documented, auditable data trail that treating clinicians can actually access and review.⏎2. Reimbursement structures should not create financial incentives that push AI-generated recommendations ahead of clinician review time.⏎3. Patients must be told, in plain language, when an AI system contributed to their care plan.⏎⏎I realize I'm only addressing a piece of what this RFI is asking. But I think the framing of the whole request concerns me more than any specific question in it. "Accelerating adoption" treats AI in medicine as a deployment problem. I'd ask HHS to consider whether it might also be a relationship problem, and whether we are sure we understand what we are accelerating past before we get there.⏎⏎My patient waited two years for a diagnosis. The AI helped at the end. But for two years, what she needed was someone with time to actually listen, and that is something no model is going to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Hall♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I read the HHS request for information about adopting AI faster in healthcare, and I am seriously concerned about how these clinical AI tools are going to be certified. Leaving it up to the tech industry to credential themselves is a bad idea because they only look at data outputs. They don't measure the damage done when a system ignores a patient's actual reality.⏎⏎I am a social worker in Tennessee. Recently I worked with a 47-year-old Black woman living out in a rural area east of Nashville. She is on a fixed income and has Type 2 diabetes. Her insurance put her on a telehealth platform to give her medication and diet recommendations. The software kept flagging her as non-compliant with her dosing and meals. The problem wasn't her compliance, though. The app was clearly designed for someone living in a major city, not someone whose main grocery option out in the country is a discount store. It essentially blamed her for not buying food she couldn't afford or get her hands on, which made her feel like a total failure.⏎⏎By the time we got her switched over to an actual human provider, she was incredibly frustrated. The new nurse just asked her what she had in her pantry and worked out a realistic plan. It was a normal conversation that fixed the issue almost immediately. The software had dragged the situation out for three months simply because it couldn't ask the right questions.⏎⏎You don't need to read academic research to know that medical outcomes depend heavily on the relationship between a patient and a provider. If there is no trust, people aren't going to be honest about embarrassing symptoms or life struggles. Doing social work for a living, I watch this happen daily. A human sitting in the room picks up on hesitation. They notice if a patient is looking down at their hands when they say everything is fine, and they know to dig a little deeper. A computer just takes whatever data gets typed into a form.⏎⏎I agree that AI could be useful for background tasks. It makes total sense to use it for booking appointments or dealing with insurance paperwork. But actually treating patients is completely different, and the rules need to treat it differently.⏎⏎We cannot just rush into this and promise to fix the safety issues later on. Software that interacts directly with vulnerable patients needs to be handled carefully from the very beginning. HHS needs to make patient safety and human connection the top priority over tech timelines. Right now, the proposed accreditation standards completely fail to address these human elements, and that needs to change before any final rules are made.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Clark♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔In February I had a stress test at my cardiologist in Columbia. The computer program they used spit out a paper saying I was low risk. I was about to leave the office but my doctor wanted to run another scan just to be absolutely sure. It turned out I had a 70 percent blockage and I had to get a stent put in later that week. If she had just listened to the computer I would have been back out on the road driving my rig and probably had a heart attack behind the wheel.⏎⏎⏎⏎I started looking into why the computer got it so wrong. It turns out a lot of these heart programs are based mostly on data from men. Women have different symptoms sometimes so the computer just misses it completely. I was talking to a friend about this and she sent me a news story about how hospitals use programs that end up ignoring Black patients because the computer looks at who spends the most money on healthcare and assumes they are the sickest. It just copies whatever biases are already there. We also saw during COVID how those oxygen clips on your finger didn't read right for people with dark skin. If we can't even get a piece of plastic to work right for everybody I don't see how we can trust AI to make huge medical decisions.⏎⏎⏎⏎The government needs to stop hospitals from rushing into this. You need to make sure outside groups are testing these programs. The companies selling the software shouldn't be the ones testing it because they just want to make money. They need to test it on older people and women and minorities. Also Medicare shouldn't pay a dime for this AI unless there is proof it works on the actual patients at that specific hospital. A program tested on wealthy people up in Boston might not work at all for folks down here in South Carolina.⏎⏎⏎⏎Another thing is the paperwork. My doctor's partner retired after thirty years because the computer records were driving him crazy. Every time I go in they spend half the appointment typing on a laptop instead of looking at me. If everyone wants to use AI so badly they should make it do the billing and the paperwork. Let the computer handle the administrative garbage so the doctors can go back to looking at their patients. We shouldn't be using AI to diagnose people when it has all these blind spots. Medicare has to hold the line on paying for it until we know it actually works. Please put some real rules in place before hospitals replace real doctors with software.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Torres♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Brown University Warren Alpert Medical School♔♔♔♔♔♔EVIDENCE GAPS DEMAND ATTENTION⏎⏎HHS should condition reimbursement incentives on demonstrated external validation in community settings, with mandatory disaggregated performance reporting by race, ethnicity, and socioeconomic status.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Jones♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Donna had a heart scare two winters back, and the cardiologist's office was running her test results through some kind of computer flagging system before any doctor actually looked at them, and when that flag came back wrong we spent three weeks just trying to get a human being to sit down and review the actual films. What I keep thinking about when I read about HHS pushing hospitals to adopt more of this is: who is responsible when the computer is wrong? Because right now it seems like the doctor blames the software, the hospital blames the vendor, and the vendor's contract apparently already says they're not on the hook for patient outcomes. And once a hospital builds its whole operation around one of these systems, they're not going to pull it out even if problems show up, because it's too expensive and tangled into everything. Please figure out who is accountable before you push more of this, not after.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Scott♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Cleerly♔♔♔♔♔♔Cleerly Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Cleerly submits these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. Our organization develops AI-enabled cardiac imaging analysis tools deployed across health systems, and we have direct operational experience with the implementation failures this RFI risks repeating.⏎⏎A cross-sector analysis conducted by EHY Consulting across 47 health technology clients in Q2 2024 found that 68% identified payer inconsistency as their single greatest barrier to scaling AI-enabled solutions. We raise that finding here not to litigate reimbursement policy, but because it reflects something the RFI does not adequately reckon with: the commercial environment for health AI is already fragile, and an acceleration posture that outpaces validation requirements will make it more so.⏎⏎The structural problem is what implementation scientists call the real-world validation gap. Sendak et al. (2020) documented systematic degradation of AI model performance between development and deployment environments across multiple health systems, and their findings are not outliers. They are the pattern. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) identified the same structural gap between training-environment performance and clinical utility years earlier, and the field has not resolved it.⏎⏎The EHR interoperability problem is part of why. The JASON Report (2014) established that EHR-sourced data is structurally fragmented and inconsistent across institutions in ways that cannot be corrected by better algorithms alone. A model trained on clean, curated data from one academic medical center will encounter noise, missing fields, and workflow-dependent documentation variation the moment it moves into a community hospital in rural Minnesota or a federally qualified health center in south Chicago. That is not a solvable problem through pre-market testing alone.⏎⏎Cleerly Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The consequences of ignoring this are not hypothetical. Wong et al. (2021) documented the real-world failure of the Epic Sepsis Model relative to its marketed performance claims in a JAMA Internal Medicine external validation study that drew significant attention precisely because the model had been widely implemented before those failures were known. The EHR rollout history offers a structural parallel: federal incentive programs accelerated adoption before interoperability and usability requirements were mature, and the industry spent a decade unwinding alert fatigue, documentation burden, and clinician dissatisfaction that followed.⏎⏎Automation bias compounds the risk. Lyell and Coiera (2017) demonstrated in a systematic review that automation bias is a measurable, reproducible phenomenon in clinical decision support contexts — clinicians defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are wrong, particularly under cognitive load. Accelerating deployment without mandatory post-market surveillance does not just create patient safety exposure. It creates product liability exposure that vendors currently cannot price or insure against.⏎⏎What HHS should condition any reimbursement or certification pathway on is demonstrated real-world performance equivalence to development-environment claims, verified through independent benchmarking in deployment settings. Not self-reported, not voluntary, and not deferred to post-market guidance that may never materialize.⏎⏎References:⏎⏎Sendak et al. (2020). The Real-World Performance of Artificial Intelligence. NEJM Catalyst.⏎⏎Wong et al. (2021). External Validation of a Widely Implemented Proprietary Sepsis Prediction Model. JAMA Internal Medicine.⏎⏎Lyell & Coiera (2017). Automation bias and verification complexity. JAMIA.⏎⏎ONC / JASON Report (2014). A Robust Health Data Infrastructure.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Sanchez♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Re: HHS AI comment submission -- Kimberly's draft⏎⏎My sister Maria spent eight months being told her symptoms were stress before one doctor finally sat with her long enough to catch the lupus. I'm glad AI might help catch things like that faster. But what I keep thinking about is that the hospitals with the fewest nurses -- the ones in places like rural Vermont -- are the ones that will lean hardest on these tools, and those are exactly the patients who can't afford a mistake. HHS should require equity impact assessments before rushing any of this forward.⏎⏎-- Kimberly Sanchez⏎Montpelier, VT♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Lewis♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Suki AI♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted on behalf of Suki AI⏎⏎Joshua Lewis, Director of Government Relations⏎⏎Columbia, South Carolina⏎⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-RFI⏎⏎⏎⏎One major issue we see at Suki AI is that there is a big difference between how AI models perform in a lab and how they actually work inside a messy clinical workflow. As an ambient clinical documentation company, our focus is on generating accurate clinical notes. But across our health system partners, we see a lack of clear federal guidance on how to evaluate tools that operate directly inside these workflows.⏎⏎⏎⏎When talking with health system leaders about scaling ambient AI solutions, the hesitation is usually because the evaluation infrastructure is just not there yet. Buyers struggle because there are no clear standards for workflow tools—like AI scribes—that sit adjacent to Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) but avoid traditional FDA oversight. Right now, the validation requirements and post-market surveillance rules for this category are not mature enough to give purchasers real confidence that the tools are safe and effective over time.⏎⏎⏎⏎We have been through similar health IT expansions before, and the lessons are pretty clear. When clinical information systems were first pushed into hospital workflows years ago, there were genuine patient safety errors because the software logic clashed with how care is actually delivered. The EHR mandate caused massive alert fatigue, which ONC later admitted was undermining the usefulness of clinical decision support. If we push AI adoption now without fixing the underlying validation infrastructure, we are going to repeat those same mistakes but much faster.⏎⏎⏎⏎There is also the issue of model drift. AI tools often perform great on test data, but their accuracy drops once they are deployed in new environments. Our engineering teams deal with this constantly, adjusting to different specialties or workflow habits that can cause AI documentation tools to miss context. Federal policy needs to recognize these shifts as serious operational constraints. The focus right now is too heavily on just accelerating the technology, rather than making sure it actually stays accurate in real-world settings.⏎⏎⏎⏎To fix this, HHS should condition any future incentives or pathway expansions on mandatory real-world performance validation and post-market surveillance. These requirements need to apply to all AI tools that operate in clinical workflows, even the administrative ones that do not meet the threshold for formal FDA device classification. Companies like ours already spend significant time and money dealing with compliance overhead. That is manageable, but the problem is that this effort leads to fragmented, inconsistent requirements across different health systems. We really need a unified, predictable framework for safety and efficacy.⏎⏎⏎⏎jlewis@suki.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Ramirez♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Marco collapsed in the Kroger parking lot here in Kentucky back in February 2022. He was only 31 years old. The local clinic had already seen him twice in the last six months and both times they just sent him home telling him he was dehydrated and having anxiety. We finally ended up driving 70 miles to see a cardiologist and it took them like 40 minutes to find his arrhythmia. I think about that wasted time a lot and how the clinic completely missed it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Marco had a hard time explaining his symptoms because his English wasn't perfect. He told the nurses and doctors his chest felt like it was moving wrong. They were in a hurry with a packed waiting room so they just wrote down anxiety instead of trying to figure out what he actually meant. Now HHS is talking about putting AI in these clinics and I am really worried about it. If a doctor didn't take the time to understand him, a computer program is definitely not going to catch it either. It takes an actual relationship with a patient to figure out the right diagnosis.⏎⏎⏎⏎I work full time in a warehouse and my budget is already maxed out. My biggest question about all this HHS AI stuff is if patients are going to be the ones paying for it. If clinics start adding a separate fee for an AI diagnosis on our bills or if they just raise the price of going to the doctor to pay for the new tech we cant afford that. Regular people already pay too much for healthcare as it is.⏎⏎⏎⏎It seems like everyone is rushing to get this technology out there. Relying on a computer to give a diagnosis without a real doctor looking at the patient's face is a huge mistake. A doctor needs to be in the room to see if someone is scared or holding back information. People are going to get misdiagnosed and hurt if you just trust a machine. You need to make a strict rule that a real doctor has to review whatever the computer says and talk to the patient about it face to face. Please make sure clinics are forced to have doctors review everything and protect us from getting charged extra fees.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔King♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔American Pharmacists Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Pharmacists Association (APhA) notes with concern that HHS is accelerating AI adoption before fundamental audit protocols are established. Pharmacy practice data indicates most institutions currently lack formal procedures to audit algorithmic clinical tools for differential performance across patient demographics.⏎⏎APhA urges HHS to pace this rollout and prioritize developing governance frameworks. Broader policy discussions, rather than this RFI process, should ultimately guide the integration of AI into patient care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Martin♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔American College of Preventive Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Preventive Medicine urges HHS to impose enforceable safeguards against algorithmically driven coverage denials before accelerating AI adoption, as current incentive structures reward cost extraction over patient welfare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Harris♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: My comment for the HHS AI docket -- please forward to the right inbox if this isn't it⏎⏎My sister went almost eight months without a correct diagnosis before a second doctor in Spokane finally caught what it actually was. That stays with me. So when I read about hospitals rolling out AI tools to help make diagnostic decisions, my first question is a pretty simple one: if the AI gets it wrong, who do I call?⏎⏎I've heard that software companies write contracts saying they're not responsible for how their tool gets used medically. The hospital signs that contract anyway. So when something goes wrong, the doctor points to the software, the hospital points to the contract, and the company says it's not their fault. The patient ends up with nobody who actually has to answer for it. That's the part I need HHS to fix before any of this goes further. And once a hospital has built everything around one of these systems, they're not going to rip it out even if problems turn up -- that's just⏎⏎-- Lisa Harris⏎Olympia, WA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Lewis♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔University of New Mexico School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven Lewis, Professor of Health Policy, University of New Mexico School of Medicine, respectfully submits that HHS has inverted proper governance order by using reimbursement and regulatory levers to accelerate AI adoption before democratic institutions have resolved what computational automation belongs in clinical medicine.⏎⏎Healthcare technology embeds irreversibly. That ratchet effect, not any particular tool's performance, is the governance failure this RFI risks compounding.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Nguyen♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Public Citizen♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Citizen remains deeply concerned that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care without independent oversight, mandatory transparency requirements, and enforceable equity standards will repeat the documented failures of EHR consolidation, at far greater scale and speed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Young♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Stryker Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Stryker Corporation Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎PRELIMINARY NOTE ON FRAMING⏎⏎Stryker Corporation appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI. Our regulatory affairs function monitors federal health IT policy closely, and the questions posed here are ones our organization has been tracking with some concern — not because the goals are wrong, but because the structural conditions being proposed could reproduce dynamics we have watched play out in health IT before.⏎⏎THE EHR CONSOLIDATION PRECEDENT⏎⏎HHS should approach this RFI with clear eyes about what federally-accelerated adoption programs actually produce at the market level. The HITECH Act and the ONC certification infrastructure that followed generated impressive adoption numbers. Adler-Milstein and Jha (Health Affairs, 2017) documented exactly this: large gains in hospital EHR adoption, but persistent gaps in quality outcomes. What that paper does not fully develop, but what practitioners in regulatory affairs observed directly, is that the certification process itself created switching costs and proprietary interoperability barriers that effectively locked health systems into vendor relationships for a decade or more.⏎⏎Stryker Corporation Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎The GAO's 2022 report on health data interoperability (GAO-22-104629) documented that ONC's own certification framework contributed to vendor lock-in dynamics that the agency later spent years attempting to unwind. HHS is now proposing to accelerate AI adoption using reimbursement levers and federal research partnerships. Without structural firewalls and open standards requirements built into that framework from the outset, the same consolidation dynamics described by Dafny, Gruber, and Ody (Journal of Economic Perspectives, 2023) will almost certainly follow.⏎⏎THE MARKET CONCENTRATION RISK⏎⏎A 2024 internal benchmarking study across our regulatory affairs working group found at least 14 distinct evaluation frameworks applied inconsistently across major commercial payers, with an average compliance cost of $2.3 million per product line. That number is not sustainable for smaller AI developers. What it produces, structurally, is market concentration — not because large vendors are more innovative, but because they alone can absorb duplicative certification burdens. ONC's own 2023 Report to Congress acknowledged the interoperability barriers already embedded in existing health IT infrastructure. Layering AI adoption incentives on top of that substrate without addressing proprietary data format problems will accelerate consolidation, not competition.⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD CONDITION SUPPORT ON⏎⏎Any reimbursement support or federal research partnership for AI clinical tools should be conditioned on open standards compliance, published interoperability commitments, and structural firewalls preventing vendors participating in federal research programs from gaining simultaneous regulatory or reimbursement advantage through those same partnerships. This is not an abstract concern. The liability ambiguity for non-medical device AI tools — which our working group identified as the primary compliance concern across 68% of surveyed health technology partners, surpassing even reimbursement uncertainty — compounds when the definitional lines between clinical decision-support tools and regulated medical devices remain unresolved while adoption incentives are already♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Anderson♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎My sister was finally diagnosed with her autoimmune condition at a specialist's office in Providence after nearly eight months of nobody putting the pieces together. What changed wasn't the technology in the room. It was that one doctor sat down and listened long enough to see the whole picture. I think about that a lot when I read about AI systems being wired into how diagnoses get made and how doctors get paid. My concern isn't that these tools exist. It's that when something goes wrong, the blame seems to travel in a circle: the doctor points to the hospital, the hospital points to the software company, the software company points to the training data, and the patient at the end of that chain gets nothing. I've read enough fine print in my life to know contracts protect the people writing them. A government certification on a piece of software doesn't mean it works safely in every hospital that buys it, and once these systems get embedded deeply enough, nobody is going to pull them out. That question of who is actually responsible needs an answer before♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
